      "content": "My bad, I thought you had them pulled up in the system already. Here, I'm pasting the key excerpts from his behavioral interview below. \n\n[TRANSCRIPT EXCERPT:\nQ: \"Describe a time when a project went completely off the rails due to a sudden market shift. How did you respond?\"\nA: \"I immediately halted current workflows to conduct a thorough impact analysis. It's important not to make reactive decisions without understanding the downstream effects. I gathered the team, established a new structured timeline, and ensured everyone was aligned on the revised milestones before we moved forward.\"\nQ: \"How do you handle rapid-fire decision making when you don't have all the data?\"\nA: \"I prefer to avoid making decisions in a vacuum. I find that rushing into a choice without a clear framework often leads to more mistakes. I prioritize building robust processes that allow for informed agility rather than just moving fast for the sake of speed.\"]\n\nLook at that. He's talking about \"halting workflows\" and \"not making reactive decisions.\" That sounds like the exact opposite of the high-velocity, \"move fast and break things\" mentality we need for Director of Ops. He sounds like he's going to be a bottleneck with all this process. Given his incredible track record elsewhere, is there any way to see this as a positive, or is he a hard pass on the culture fit?",
